## ⚙️ Requirements

Before running the project, install:

* Node.js
* npm
* MongoDB

Check your Node.js and npm versions:

```bash
node -v
npm -v
```

## 📥 Installation

Clone the repository:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/portfolio-builder.git
```

Move into the project:

```bash
cd portfolio-builder
```

## 🔧 Backend Setup

Open a terminal:

```bash
cd backend
```

Install dependencies:

```bash
npm install
```

Create a `.env` file inside the `backend` folder.

Example:

```env
PORT=5000
MONGO_URI=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/portfolio_builder
JWT_SECRET=your_secret_key
JWT_EXPIRES_IN=7d
```

> Never upload your actual `.env` file to GitHub.

Start the backend:

```bash
npm run dev
```

If your project does not have a development script, use:

```bash
node server.js
```

The backend will normally run at:

```text
http://localhost:5000
```

## 🎨 Frontend Setup

Open another terminal:

```bash
cd frontend
```

Install dependencies:

```bash
npm install
```

Start the frontend:

```bash
npm run dev
```

The frontend will normally run at:

```text
http://localhost:5173
```

## 🔐 Environment Variables

The backend requires environment variables.

Create:

```text
backend/.env
```

Example:

```env
PORT=5000
MONGO_URI=your_mongodb_connection_string
JWT_SECRET=your_secret_key
JWT_EXPIRES_IN=7d
```

Do not commit `.env` to GitHub.

## 🗄️ Database

This project uses **MongoDB** as its database.

For local development, the default connection can be:

```text
mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/portfolio_builder
```

You can also use MongoDB Atlas by replacing the `MONGO_URI` value with your Atlas connection string.

## ▶️ Running the Complete Project

You need two terminals.

### Terminal 1 — Backend

```bash
cd backend
npm install
npm run dev
```

### Terminal 2 — Frontend

```bash
cd frontend
npm install
npm run dev
```

Then open the frontend URL shown in your terminal.

## 🧪 Development

If you make changes to the source code, restart the development server if necessary.

Dependencies are installed using:

```bash
npm install
```

## 📌 Important Notes

* Do not upload `.env`.
* Do not upload `node_modules`.
* Keep your database credentials private.
* Anyone cloning the project needs to create their own `.env` file.
* Run `npm install` inside both `backend` and `frontend` before starting the application.

